` as `str | os.PathLike[str] | cabc.Iterable[str | os.PathLike[str]] | None`. The body wraps `filename` in a tuple when it is `str` or `os.PathLike`. The docstring gained the \"accepts a path or any iterable of paths\" sentence and a `versionchanged:: 8.5.0` entry.\n- **`src/click/_termui_impl.py`**: `Editor.edit_files` takes `Iterable[str | os.PathLike[str]]`. The body is unchanged, since `subprocess.Popen` accepts path objects in its args.\n- **`CHANGES.md`**: added the entry at the end of the Unreleased list.\n\nNotes:\n- The spec says \"both `@t.overload` signatures,\" but only one overload has a `filename` parameter. I changed that overload and the implementation, which are the two places `filename` appears.\n- Before the termui change, `test_edit_pathlib[single]` failed with `TypeError: 'PosixPath' object is not iterable`. It passes now.\n- Two of the new annotation lines are 91 characters, over the 88-character default that ruff uses. I kept the spec's exact text. A `ruff format` run would reflow them. I did not run the formatter or the typing tests in `tests/typing/typing_edit.py`, since the spec lists only the pytest command.", "ttft_ms": 2338, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 30297, "uuid": "2d638120-8235-4f22-b195-6754af27174d", "ttft_stream_ms": 2099, "time_to_request_ms": 436, "first_content_frame_ms": 2099, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0}
